    severityLevels: ["Level 1 - sodium retained in floors/pits/retention tanks with limited concrete contact: slow reaction, modest hydrogen, no structural concern.", "Level 2 - sustained sodium-concrete reaction: a hot sodium pool on concrete drives the exothermic reaction with concrete water, surging hydrogen generation and local concrete erosion/penetration.", "Level 3 - structural degradation plus flammable cloud: the reaction degrades concrete load-carrying capability and accumulates hydrogen to a flammable concentration in the containment atmosphere.", "Level 4 - hydrogen deflagration or deflagration-to-detonation transition that loads the containment thermally and by overpressure."],
    affectedAreas: ["Reactor-building and sodium-boiler-building concrete floors, pits, cells and structural walls", "Containment atmosphere and confinement region", "Concrete around the primary tank, cavity liner and equipment cells", "Any compartment where leaked sodium can pool on or penetrate to concrete"],
